{
  "answers": [
    {
      "question_id": "Q1",
      "question": "What event or condition triggers the decision to transfer a wafer sample for evaluation — for example, is it a scheduled review cycle, an ad hoc request from an engineer, or a signal from a monitoring system?",
      "answer": "The process starts when a person needing the sample identifies which specific wafer is required and articulates why it is needed for testing. This act of identification and purpose definition is what initiates everything — it is essentially a demand-driven trigger rather than a scheduled or automated signal."
    },
    {
      "question_id": "Q2",
      "question": "After evaluation, what specific criteria or outcome determines that a sample is no longer needed and should be flagged for disposal?",
      "answer": "After testing concludes, the engineer handling verification updates the sample's standing. A disposal candidate is flagged when no retention obligation applies to that sample. If conditions requiring the sample to be kept are absent, it becomes eligible for a disposal request. The specific numeric or technical thresholds are not defined in the available information."
    },
    {
      "question_id": "Q3",
      "question": "Who has the authority to approve a disposal request, and are there multiple approval levels depending on the sample type or quantity?",
      "answer": "A designated approver authorizes disposal requests. The same approver role also greenlights the initial movement of samples. Only one approval tier is described — there is no mention of escalating approval levels based on sample type or quantity."
    },
    {
      "question_id": "Q4",
      "question": "Which roles or job functions are responsible for each major step — initiating the transfer, performing the evaluation, submitting the disposal request, and executing the disposal?",
      "answer": "The person requesting the sample initiates and submits the movement request. The approver authorizes it. A sample manager physically confirms and relocates the specimen. A verification engineer updates status post-testing and files the disposal request. The approver then finalizes and logs the disposal outcome."
    },
    {
      "question_id": "Q5",
      "question": "How is the physical transfer of wafer samples carried out between locations — is it done manually by staff, via an automated transport system, or through a third-party logistics process?",
      "answer": "The sample manager physically confirms the specimen and then moves it to the designated destination. This points to a manual, staff-executed relocation. No automated conveyor system or external logistics provider is mentioned in the available information."
    },
    {
      "question_id": "Q6",
      "question": "Once a disposal is completed, how and where is that outcome recorded — for example, is a system of record updated, and is any confirmation sent back to the requester?",
      "answer": "When the approver finalizes disposal, a disposal record is created and the sample's standing is updated accordingly. A physical confirmation note is also produced. Whether an automated notification is sent back to the original requester is not explicitly specified."
    }
  ]
}
